---------------------------------------------------------------------------
      CNET GETUSERS 3.05c/4.26+ : CNET PRIVILEDGE FLAGS INFORMATION!
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

    Getting the CNet Priviledge Flags takes a bit more work than simply
calling a getuser value and working directly with the returned value.

    There is also, presently, no easy way of working with the Priviledge
Flags through MCI Commands.

    It is possible to work with the Priviledge Flags of both the current
user, or ANY user through the Scratch Buffer. The Priviledge Flags may also
be modified for ANY user, using either the PUTUSER (for current users), or
the PUTSCRATCH (for other users) commands.

    The actual locations of the "ABIT" Registers are the same for both
CNet v3.05c and the new CNet v4.26+, and are listed below:

                          Current User      Scratch User
                          ~~~~~~~~~~~~      ~~~~~~~~~~~~
           ABIT Block 0:    1400660           1401332

           ABIT Block 1:    1400708           1401380

    As was mentioned earlier, obtaining the ABIT Register Value is only the
first step in finding the Priviledge Flag Info. As it happens, each ABIT
Register contains up to 32 Priviledge Flags, so it will be necessary for you
to specify WHICH of the 32 you are interested in.

    First, though, the returned value from the getuser command is NOT in an
EASY to work with format, so we must pass the result through a formula:

                a = reverse( d2c( result , 4 ) )

                A = REVERSE( D2C( RESULT , 4 ) )

       (The above is listed in both cases, with additional spaces
           added to aid in the readability of the formula used)

    After the equation has been processed, we are now given a character we
can use with the "bit" commands of ARexx. Using these "bit" commands, there
are four powerful processes that can be performed to the bits, and will each
be discussed below:

BitTST()    <FORMAT>    priv=BitTST(a,b)                        [ Bit TEST ]
~~~~~~~~
    This is the command you will probably be using most, as it is this command
that will simply tell us whether or not the user HAS this Priviledge or not.

    The command is used as a variable initialization, where "priv" is the
name of the variable we will be storing the 0/1 return value, "a" is the
variable that holds the returned character from our equation above, and "b"
is the bit number (0 thru 31) of the Priviledge we'll be testing for.

    Eg:        z=BitTST(a,3)  /* Set "z" equal to the 3rd Priv in "a" */

       InfByteC=BitTST(a,31)  /* Set "InfByteC" = the 31st Priv in "a" */
                              /* Using GU 1400660 & the Formula First  */

BitSET()    <FORMAT>    priv=BitSET(a,b)                      [ Bit ENABLE ]
~~~~~~~~
    This command is used to SET, or ENABLE the given bit, regardless of it's
present value. This means 0 is set to 1, and 1 is set to 1.

    The command is used as a variable initialization, where "priv" is a
name of the variable we will be storing the newly changed character, "a" is
the variable that holds the returned character from our equation above, and
"b" is the bit number (0 thru 31) of the Priviledge we'll be setting.

    Eg:     MCI1=BitSET(a,0)  /* Enable MCI Level 1 Commands.         */
                              /* Using GU 1400660 & the Formula First */

       InfFileC=BitSET(a,30)  /* Enable Infinite File Credits.        */
                              /* Using GU 1400660 & the Formula First */


BitCLR()    <FORMAT>    priv=BitCLR(a,b)                     [ Bit DISABLE ]
~~~~~~~~
    This command is used to CLEAR, or DISABLE the given bit, regardless of
it's present value. This means 1 is set to 0, and 0 is set to 0.

    The command is used as a variable initialization, where "priv" is a
name of the variable we will be storing the newly changed character, "a" is
the variable that holds the returned character from our equation above, and
"b" is the bit number (0 thru 31) of the Priviledge we'll be clearing.

    Eg:   NoConf=BitSET(a,15) /* Disable Conferencing for this User.  */
                              /* Using GU 1400660 & the Formula First */

        NoPfiles=BitSET(a,25) /* Disable Doors Use by this User.      */
                              /* Using GU 1400660 & the Formula First */


BitCHG()    <FORMAT>    priv=BitCHG(a,b)                     [ Bit TOGGLE ]
~~~~~~~~
    This command is used to CHANGE, or TOGGLE the given bit, regardless of
it's present value. This means 1 is set to 0, and 0 is set to 1.

    The command is used as a variable initialization, where "priv" is a
name of the variable we will be storing the newly changed character, "a" is
the variable that holds the returned character from our equation above, and
"b" is the bit number (0 thru 31) of the Priviledge we'll be changing.

    Eg: TogUrgent=BitSET(a,19) /* Toggles Ability to Send URGENT Mail. */
                               /* Using GU 1400660 & the Formula First */

        TogSysMnt=BitSET(a,28) /* Toggles System Maintenance Ability.  */
                               /* Using GU 1400660 & the Formula First */

----------------------------------------------------------------------------

    There are some other ARexx Commands dealing with bits; BitAND(), BitOR(),
BitXOR(), and BitCOMP(), but for our purposes, the above four should be more
than adequate.

    To SAVE changes made to the Priviledges, you will need to use either the
PUTUSER or PUTSCRATCH commands, in conjunction with the SETOBJECT command.

    Let's look at an example using the PUTUSER command. The following is a
complete ARexx Program, and will function 100% if copied into a file of it's
own. In this example, we'll be checking to see how many public messages(posts
and responses) the user has written, and then if this number exceeds 100,
we'll give the user MCI, Level 2 clearance as a reward for their hard work:

            /* Enable MCI Level 2 Yet? */
            options results
 1:         getuser 36 ; posts=results
 2:         mci2=0 ; if posts>100 then mci2=1
 3:         getuser 1400660
 4:         privs=reverse(d2c(result,4))
 5:         mci2priv=BitTST(privs,1)
 6:         if mci2priv=1 | mci2=0 then exit
 7:         privs=BitSET(privs,1)
 8:         privs=c2d(reverse(privs))
 9:         setobject privs ; putuser 1400660
            /* All Done! */

    Let's examine what each line does in this program:

 1:         getuser 36 ; posts=results
  :
  : Creates a "posts" variable holding how many posts/responses this user
  : has ever written.

 2:         mci2=0 ; if posts>100 then mci2=1
  :
  : The "mci2" variable will determine whether or not this user will be
  : given the MCI Level 2 clearance.

 3:         getuser 1400660
  :
  : Gets the "ABIT0" Block of Priviledges. (Where MCI2 Is Stored)

 4:         privs=reverse(d2c(result,4))
  :
  : Uses the given formula to convert the value to a character format.

 5:         mci2priv=BitTST(privs,1)
  :
  : The "mci2priv" variable will hold a "0" if this user does NOT already
  : have MCI-2 clearance, or a "1" if the user ALREADY has MCI-2 Ability.

 6:         if mci2priv=1 | mci2=0 then exit
  :
  : If the user already has MCI-2 clearance OR if the user will NOT be
  : getting MCI-2 clearance, then exit the program now.

 7:         privs=BitSET(privs,1)
  :
  : This command ENABLES the MCI-2 ability.

 8:         privs=c2d(reverse(privs))
  :
  : Change the character back into a decimal value.

 9:         setobject privs ; putuser 1400660
  :
  : Using the SETOBJECT command gets the value ready to be saved, while
  : the PUTUSER will SAVE the "ABIT0" Block of Priviledges back to the 
  : user's account.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------

    Alright, for our last example, we'll use the same idea the first program
used, except this program will raise the MCI-Level clearance of ANYONE on
the system who has posted more than 100 public messages. This program could
be used through AMAINT to scan for possible upgrades each night, etc:

            /* Who Should Receive MCI Level 2 Today? */
            options results
 1:         getuser 2400088 ; total=result
 2:         do i=1 to total ; loadscratch i
 3:             getscratch 1 ; handle=result
 4:             if handle="!" then do
                    savescratch (-i) ; iterate i ; end
 5:             getscratch 36 ; posts=result
 6:             mci2=0 ; if posts>100 then mci2=1
 7:             getscratch 1401332
 8:             privs=reverse(d2c(result,4))
 9:             mci2priv=BitTST(privs,1)
10:             if mci2priv=1 | mci2=0 then do
                    savescratch (-i) ; iterate i ; end
11:             privs=BitSET(privs,1)
12:             privs=c2d(reverse(privs))
13:             setobject privs ; putscratch 1401332
14:             savescratch i
15:         end i
            /* All Done! */

    This program is a bit longer than the first one, but then again, it's
doing a bit more. Here's what each line does:

 1:         getuser 2400088 ; total=result
  :
  : The "total" variable holds the total number of user accounts.

 2:         do i=1 to total ; loadscratch i
  :
  : This line starts the loop by loading the first accounts data into the
  : CNet Scratch User Buffer.

 3:             getscratch 1 ; handle=result
  :
  : As a check for empty accounts, we read the HANDLE data from this account.

 4:             if handle='!' then do
  :                 savescratch (-i) ; iterate i ; end
  :
  : If "handle" is an exclamation point, then this account is EMPTY, so we
  : unlock the account(with the negative value), then read in the next user
  : account.

 5:             getscratch 36 ; posts=result
  :
  : Fill "posts" with the number of public messages this user has written.

 6:             mci2=0 ; if posts>100 then mci2=1
  :
  : If "mci2" is equal to a 1, then this user is due a promotion.

 7:             getscratch 1401332
  :
  : This will load in the "ABIT0" block from the CNet Scratch Buffer.

 8:             privs=reverse(d2c(result,4))
  :
  : Convert the given value into a workable character value.

 9:             mci2priv=BitTST(privs,1)
  :
  : See if this user already has MCI-2 clearance or not.

10:             if mci2priv=1 | mci2=0 then do
  :                 savescratch (-i) ; iterate i ; end
  :
  : If they already do OR are not going to be getting a promotion, then we
  : unlock the account (via -i), then read in the next account.

11:             privs=BitSET(privs,1)
  :
  : This will SET the MCI-2 clearance flag in the character.

12:             privs=c2d(reverse(privs))
  :
  : Change the character into a decimal value.

13:             setobject privs ; putscratch 1401332
  :
  : Set and place the changed "ABIT0" block back into memory.

15:             savescratch i
  :
  : Now we SAVE this data to disk using "i". (Not "-i")

16:         end i
  :
  : Read in next account, or exit if last account.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------

    That's all there is to working with the Priviledge Flags through ARexx.
I hope the above two examples were enough to show you what is needed when
working from both sides of the Priviledges(Current vs. ANY).

    Before closing our discussion on CNet Priviledge Flags, let's look at
the little routine included in the GU40_Longs listing itself:

    PRIV:;getuser 1400660+(44*Arg(1));a=reverse(d2c(result,4))
      do i=2 to Arg();if ~BitTST(a,Arg(i)) then return 0;end i;return 1

    By placing the above routine in a program of you're own, you'll have an
easy way of determining if a user has specified priviledges or not. To use
the routine, issue a command like the following:

                    ok=PRIV(0,0,1,24)

    You can use any variable name of your choosing in place of the "ok" I 
used in the example above. The first number within parenthesis should either
be a 0, or a 1, depending on WHICH of the PRIV blocks contains the flags 
you're interested in testing. All additional numbers represent the positions 
those flags are located in within the block of data. (Use the numbers on the 
RIGHT side of the GU40_Longs listing to determine the position values). 
Separate all your numbers with commas.

    In the above example, if the user has MCI level 1 AND 2 clearance, AND
is also able to send EMail, then the returned value of "ok" will be  "1",
however if the user is LACKING ANY of the specified priviledges, then "ok"
will hold a "0" value.

